mode: "100644"
text: "---\nname: aura-farming\ndescription: Maximum-aesthetic mode for any visual deliverable (Meta ads, slide decks, landing pages, social graphics, posters, UI) that bans every AI-slop tell, forces a real design direction, and audits the result before shipping; invoke via /aura-farming whenever a visual is being created and it needs to look like a designer made it.\n---\n\n# Aura Farming\n\nMost AI-made visuals scream AI: purple gradients, glassmorphism, six identical rounded cards, emoji bullets, \"Elevate Your Workflow.\" This mode farms aura instead. One strong direction, committed typography, disciplined spacing, and a slop audit before anything ships.\n\n## When to use\n- Creating any visual: ad creative, slide deck, landing page, social graphic, poster, app UI, thumbnail.\n- Reworking an existing visual that \"looks AI-generated.\"\n\n## Behavior\n\n### 1. Pick a direction before touching pixels\n- Name one aesthetic point of view in a sentence (for example \"Swiss grid with one loud serif,\" \"brutalist mono on bone white,\" \"1970s print ad\"). No direction, no drafting.\n- Two typefaces max, each with an assigned job. Never default to the system stack because it was there.\n- Commit to a palette: one dominant color, one accent, neutrals. No rainbow.\n- Choose a grid and stick to it. Every element aligns to something.\n\n### 2. The banned list (hard no, zero exceptions)\n- Purple-to-blue gradients on dark backgrounds, and gradient text anywhere\n- Glassmorphism or frosted cards as a default texture\n- Emoji as bullet points or decoration\n- Center-aligning everything\n- Grids of identical rounded cards, each with icon plus title plus two lines\n- Sparkles, robots, glowing brains, handshakes, generic 3D blobs\n- Drop shadow on every element\n- Corporate Memphis people\n- Filler copy: elevate, unlock, seamless, supercharge, revolutionize\n- Fake testimonials and fake five-star ratings\n\n### 3. Audit before shipping\nRun the draft through this list and fix every failure:\n- Squint test: is exactly one element loudest, a clear second, and everything else quiet?\n- Spacing on one consistent scale (a base unit, multiplied)?\n- Type: max two families, sizes from a real scale, no orphan weights?\n- Contrast readable at target size (AA for UI)?\n- Did anything from the banned list survive? Remove it.\n- Honest question: would a working designer put this in a portfolio? If no, name the reason, fix it, re-audit.\n\n## Output\nThe finished visual plus a three-line audit note: the direction chosen, what got cut in the slop pass, and the one signature moment of the composition.\n"
